\title{illume: A Unified Engine for Exploration and Frequentist Inference}
\description{
A frequentist regression workflow aimed at \strong{inference} rather than
prediction, together with the exploratory steps that precede it. One engine
fits gaussian, binomial, Poisson, negative binomial, multinomial, beta,
ordinal, zero-inflated, hurdle and survival models, so every model returns
the same object and shares the same methods and diagnostics.
}
\section{What problem this solves}{

Fitting a range of models in R usually means a range of packages, each with
its own object, its own \code{anova()} semantics, its own \code{predict()} arguments and
its own idea of what a diagnostic plot is. \code{illume} fits them with one engine,
so the interface does not change as the model gets more complicated -- from
\code{y ~ x} through to a multinomial mixed model with smooths and correlated
errors.

Where an exact small-sample reference exists it is used: a gaussian model
with no random effects reports t and F tests agreeing with \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}},
rather than the large-sample approximations the Laplace machinery would
otherwise give.

The \strong{multinomial} family closes a specific gap. \code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} handles
unordered categorical outcomes but has no random effects; \code{\link[lme4:glmer]{lme4::glmer()}} and
\code{glmmTMB} have rich random-effect machinery but no multinomial family; \code{brms}
fits the full model but is far slower and answers a different inferential
question.
}

\section{Families}{

\code{"gaussian"}, \code{"binomial"}, \code{"poisson"}, \code{"nbinom"}, \code{"beta"},
\code{"multinomial"}, the ordinal families, and the survival families
\code{"weibull"}, \code{"lognormal"}, \code{"loglogistic"} and the flexible \code{"rp"}; see
\code{\link[=ilm_family]{ilm_family()}}. A count or a proportion can have a zero part, inflated or
hurdle (\code{ziformula}). By default the family is read off the response, and
the fit says which it chose and why. Tweedie is out of scope, because
\code{glmmTMB} covers it well.
}

\section{How the model is written}{

With \code{J} outcome categories the model has \code{C = J - 1} free linear predictors.
\code{illume} uses \strong{sum-to-zero} coding across categories, so each coefficient is
that category's deviation from the average across categories, \emph{not} a contrast
against a baseline category. This differs from \code{\link[nnet:multinom]{nnet::multinom()}} and \code{brms},
which use baseline-category coding, and matters when reading output: see
\code{\link[=ilm_coef_table]{ilm_coef_table()}}.
}

\section{Why the diagnostics are prominent}{

This model class fails \emph{quietly}. A fit can return entirely plausible
coefficients while its covariance matrix is unusable, so the standard errors
are meaningless even though nothing looks wrong. Every fit therefore carries a
table of checks (see \code{fit$checks}), each with a status, a plain-language
reason, and a suggested remedy. \code{\link[=summary.ilm_model]{summary.ilm_model()}} prints them.
}

\section{Two spellings}{

Every exported function whose name starts \code{ilm_} also answers to \code{iml_}, an
easy transposition to type: \code{iml_model()} is \code{\link[=ilm_model]{ilm_model()}} itself, not a
wrapper, and opens the same help page.
}

